sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Cyclotrons for Radionuclide Production","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/cyclotrons"}

definition:
    A compact circular particle accelerator invented by Ernest O. Lawrence in 1930. Electric fields kick charged particles faster with each pass while a magnetic field bends them into a widening spiral until they exit at high energy. Cyclotrons are workhorses of nuclear medicine: hospital-based machines bombard targets to make short-lived positron emitters such as fluorine-18 for PET scans. (Technetium-99m, the most-used diagnostic isotope, comes mainly from reactors instead.) Lawrence won the 1939 Nobel Prize in Physics for the cyclotron.
